---
// Two of the three labels here used to point at pages the build does not emit: `dist/client` contains
// index.html, chat/index.html and 404.html only, and public/ has no redirect rules — so "Schedule" and
// "Members" rendered the 404 page in production, on main as well as here. The operator ruled: drop them.
//
// What the row costs now, measured on the built page by `node design/nav-fit.mjs` — the same needed-vs-
// available arithmetic `check:render` runs, per label @measured 2026-11-14 (Linux native, Firefox 159.0,
// faces settled): the mark is 97px, `Schedule` 59, `Chat` 29, `Dates` 36, `Join` 24, `Attend` 42,
// `Ways in` 47, `Sign up` 47, `Meet` 32, `Visit` 28, `Get involved` 77. A row at the 320px floor (D114)
// has 276px of content box and 16px gaps, so the label set needs 217px and has 59px of headroom: a fourth
// label costs a gap plus a word no wider than 43px. `Schedule` + `Chat` + `Ways in` is 280 — four pixels
// over the floor, and `flex-nowrap` means that shows up as a failed probe rather than a silent wrap.
// Keeping `Ways in` therefore means paying for it with `Schedule` -> `Dates`: 97 + 36 + 29 + 47 + 3 gaps
// = 257, 19px inside the floor.
//
// Headroom, as it was measured before the schedule page existed, kept so a later reader does not re-argue it:
// the row was the mark plus `Chat` (171px of the 276 the floor offers), and `Schedule` at 57.6px would have
// fitted. It did, and 2026-11-07 spent that room on it (`src/pages/schedule.astro` emits
// `dist/client/schedule/index.html`) while `Members` stayed out for the reason D117 gives - no members page -
// not for lack of width. What the old note got wrong is the count: the floor had room for one more path, and
// the probe now says so every run.
// 2026-11-14 (S034, D308): the third path is the join page. The label is the page's own heading - "Ways in" -
// and `Schedule` came down to `Dates` to pay for it, because the floor cannot hold both words and the shorter
// candidates (`Join` 24, `Visit` 28, `Meet` 32, `Attend` 42 at 1px of margin) say less than the page does.
// Every label behind it is a page the build emits (D117), and `check:render` reports the shipped row at
// **257 needed of 276 offered** at 320 @measured 2026-11-14.
const navItems = [
  { href: '/schedule', label: 'Dates' },
  { href: '/chat', label: 'Chat' },
  { href: '/join', label: 'Ways in' },
];
---

<!-- Round 17 (MS03), operator ruling 2026-11-09 (S017 B1): the bar is chrome by an edge, not by a ground.
     Measured on the built page before the change (2026-11-08, design/grill/edge-probe.mjs): the header paints
     no ground of its own - its computed background is the page's paper (layer contrast 1.00, OKLab distance 0),
     so nothing in the bar separates it from the page. Every ground the palette has was priced and refused:
     panel 1.04, tint 1.10, warm tint 1.11 (drops `rule` to 2.74 against its own 3.0 bar), warm band 1.20
     (`rule` 2.51), gold fill fails outright (nav ink 2.5). The ratified inset ring is the one device that
     clears the graphic bar for free in height: 3.01 against the paper it stands on, bar still 1228x72.
     `shadow-e2` paints 1.78 - a layer, not an edge - and is held back until the bar actually moves (sticky,
     parked in PLAN.md §Parked with its trigger). An inset ring is not a border (D103), so check:design's
     no-border rule still holds.

     S027 / D295 (operator ruling B1, 2026-11-13): the bar is chrome by its **ring alone**. `bg-paper` was
     deleted from the header class - it declared a ground identical to the page behind it (re-measured by
     `node design/round17.mjs --only shipped`, 2026-11-13), so it painted nothing and invited a future reader
     to think the bar has a surface of its own. The ring is the whole device; sticky is still parked. -->
<header class="inset-shadow-rule">
  <nav class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-4">
    <!-- D100 shape B, re-measured on the built artifact by `npm run check:render`: one row at every
         width, `flex-wrap: nowrap`, one type tier (text-label) at every width, 16px gaps, and link
         targets 32px tall (16px line box + py-2). D100 rejected a 13px target, so the pad is
         load-bearing — do not shave it.

         `flex-nowrap` is the load-bearing part. The shipped nav was `flex-col` + `flex-wrap`, which is
         why the page clipped and links disappeared (A needed 397px, more than every content box this
         site ships). With nowrap, a label set that stops fitting shows up as a failed width probe
         instead of a silent re-wrap. -->
    <div class="flex flex-nowrap items-center gap-4">
      <a
        href="/"
        class="flex-shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap py-2 font-body text-body text-ink"
        aria-label="Matheapolis, home"
      >
        Matheapolis
      </a>
      <div class="flex flex-nowrap items-center gap-4">
        {navItems.map(item => (
          <a
            href={item.href}
            class="whitespace-nowrap py-2 text-label text-ink font-medium hover:text-gold"
            aria-label={`Navigate to ${item.label}`}
          >
            {item.label}
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </nav>
</header>
